course: dictionary
lesson: 13828
title: Itinerant: moving from place to place
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Itinerant describes someone who travels from place to place, often for work, instead of living in one place. You can find its meaning by reading the clues around it. «The itinerant pedlar sold ribbons at every farm on the road.» A pedlar who sells at every farm must be travelling about, so itinerant means moving from place to place.
explain: Look for clue words such as travels, moves on, each week or no fixed home. «Ruth is an itinerant farm worker. She moves with the harvest from field to field.» Moving from field to field tells you that itinerant means not staying in one place.
explain: Check your meaning with the swap test. Put «travelling from place to place» into the sentence: «The travelling pedlar sold ribbons at every farm on the road.» It still makes sense, so your meaning is right.
fact: Itinerant means travelling from place to place, often for work, instead of living in one place.
fact: As a noun, «an itinerant» is a formal word for a person who travels about like this.
fact: Long ago, itinerant preachers, pedlars, tinkers and musicians moved from town to town.
fact: Farm workers who followed the harvest were itinerant, because they moved on when each crop was picked.
fact: Itinerant is a formal word. Simpler words for it are travelling, moving about or on the move.
exercise: «The itinerant pedlar sold ribbons at every farm.» What does itinerant mean? | Selling only on Sundays | Very rich and grand | *Travelling from place to place | Living in the same house all his life
exercise: «The itinerant teacher moved on each week, never staying long in one village.» What does itinerant suggest? | A teacher who is always ill | A teacher who is very old | *A teacher who moves from village to village | A teacher who stays in one village for ever
exercise: Nadia asks: why might a writer say itinerant instead of travelling? | Because it means a fixed home | *Because it is a formal word, often used in history and stories | Because it means rich and grand | Because it means very old
test: «Ruth is an itinerant farm worker who moves with the harvest.» What does itinerant mean here? | Sleeping in the field | *Moving from place to place for work | Very hungry | Working on one farm all year
test: In formal English, what is «an itinerant»? | *A person who travels about from place to place | A type of cloud | A kind of crop | A house with a fixed address
exam: Which sentence shows someone who is itinerant? | «The farm stayed in one field all year.» | «The family has lived in the same house for fifty years.» | *«The itinerant musician played in a new town every week.»
